        "C.N.2":      "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3":      "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/",
        "C.N.5":      "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn5/",
        "I.8":        "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI8/",
        "I.26":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI26/",
        "I.47":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "III.1":      "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.16,Cor": "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII16/#cor",
        "III.18":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII18/",
        "III.27":     "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII27/",
        "IV.11":      "/elements/books/bookIV/propositions/propIV11/",
        "IV.Def.4":   "/elements/books/bookIV/definitions/defIV4/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Then, since the straight line {KL} touches the circle {ABCDE|circc} at {C}, and {FC} has been joined from the center {F} to the point of contact at {C}, therefore {FC} is perpendicular to {KL}.  Therefore each of the angles at {C} is right. [!just III.18]

For the same reason the angles at the points {B} and {D} are also right.

And, since the angle {FCK} is right, therefore the square on {FK} equals the sum of the squares on {FC} and {CK}. [!just I.47]

For the same reason the square on {FK} also equals the sum of the squares on {FB} and {BK}, so that the sum of the squares on {FC} and {CK} equals the sum of the squares on {FB} and {BK}, of which the square on {FC} equals the square on {FB}, therefore the remaining square on {CK} equals the square on {BK}. [!just I.47]

Therefore {BK} equals {CK}.

And, since {FB} equals {FC}, and {FK} is common, the two sides {BF} and {FK} equal the two sides {CF} and {FK}, and the base {BK} equals the base {CK}, therefore the angle {BFK} equals the angle {KFC}, and the angle {BKF} equals the angle {FKC|angFKC}. Therefore the angle {BFC} is double the angle {KFC}, and the angle {BKC} double the angle {FKC|angFKC}. [!just I.8]

For the same reason the angle {CFD} is also double the angle {CFL}, and the angle {DLC} double the angle {FLC|angFLC}.

Now, since the circumference {BC|arcBC} equals {CD|arcCD}, the angle {BFC} also equals the angle {CFD}. [!just III.27]

And the angle {BFC} is double the angle {KFC}, and the angle {DFC} double the angle {LFC}, therefore the angle {KFC} also equals the angle {LFC}.

But the angle {FCK} also equals the angle {FCL}, therefore {FKC} and {FLC} are two triangles having two angles equal to two angles and one side equal to one side, namely {FC} which is common to them, therefore they will also have the remaining sides equal to the remaining sides, and the remaining angle to the remaining angle, therefore the straight line {KC} equals {CL}, and the angle {FKC|angFKC} equals the angle {FLC|angFLC}. [!just I.26]

And, since {KC} equals {CL}, therefore {KL} is double {KC}.

For the same reason it can be proved that {HK} is also double {BK}.

And {BK} equals {KC}, therefore {HK} also equals {KL}.

Similarly each of the straight lines {HG}, {GM}, and {ML} can also be proved equal to each of the straight lines {HK} and {KL}, therefore the pentagon {GHKLM} is equilateral.

I say next that it is also equiangular.

For, since the angle {FKC|angFKC} equals the angle {FLC|angFLC}, and the angle {HKL} was proved double the angle {FKC|angFKC}, and the angle {KLM} double the angle {FLC|angFLC}, therefore the angle {HKL} also equals the angle {KLM}.

Similarly each of the angles {KHG}, {HGM}, and {GML} can also be proved equal to each of the angles {HKL} and {KLM}.  Therefore the five angles {GHK}, {HKL}, {KLM}, {LMG}, and {MGH} equal one another.

Therefore the pentagon {GHKLM} is equiangular.

And it was also proved equilateral, and it has been circumscribed about the circle {ABCDE|circc}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

This construction depends on the last.  First, inscribe a regular pentagon in the circle, then take tangents to the circle at the five vertices of the inscribed pentagon. Euclid didn't actually give the construction for drawing tangents, but the [Corollary](/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII16/#cor) to Proposition III.16 indicates how it should be constructed. The result will be a circumscribed pentagon.  This method generally works to create a regular circumscribed *n*-gon  given a regular inscribed *n*-gon.

Conversely, if you have a regular circumscribed *n*-gon, then you can connect the points of tangency in sequence to get a regular inscribed *n*-gon.
